Setting (from the neighbouring pages): S is a base scheme. G is an S-form of PGL(2) (or its simply connected version SG). P is the associated conic (a Brauer–Severi scheme of relative dimension 1), and M is the associated quaternion (Azumaya) algebra, with PG ≅ Aut_gr(G) ≅ Aut_Alg(M) ≅ Aut_S(P). L_1 is a 'borélien' additive subgroup: the unipotent radical of a Borel subgroup, ≅ a line bundle, corresponding to a section of P. It lives in SG and maps isomorphically into PG.

The unknowns are sections α, β of G and ρ, σ that enter only through their action on G (their images in PG), via [α,ρ] = α·ρ(α)^{-1} and [β,σ] = β·σ(β)^{-1}. Equation (132) of the 'équation des lacets' contains a factor ε_1(μ−1), which is a section of L_1. After this observation the equation takes the form (11) [β,σ]^{-1}[α,ρ] ∈ Γ(L_1), equivalently (12) [α,ρ] = [β,σ]·γ with γ ∈ Γ(L_1). This is a relation between α, β, γ.

This is a twisted, relative version of a surface-group-type relation 'product of commutators = unipotent (parabolic) element'. It belongs to the study of solutions of loop equations in forms of PGL2: representation or character-type schemes of punctured-surface groups with a unipotent condition at the puncture, in the spirit of the Longue Marche / Teichmüller-tower programme.

Passing to the image L̃_1 ⊂ PG, the auxiliary relation (40 bis) becomes (14) ε̃_1 := ρ̃σ̃^{-1} ∈ Γ(L̃_1): ρ and σ agree modulo the unipotent subgroup. Grothendieck then remarks that this comes from a well-defined section ε_1 ∈ Γ(L_1), expressible in terms of ρ, σ by a relation (16) = (40 bis): ε_1 = −λ_0 ρσ^{-1} with λ_0 ∈ Γ(G_m). Here λ_0 normalises a lift of ρσ^{-1} from PG to SG (or to M^*) so that it becomes the unipotent ε_1. An inserted remark concerns (non-)commutativity of the relevant map ('celle qui nous intéresse le plus').

The marginal NB (only partly legible) says λ_0 is uniquely determined by ρ, σ under a condition on 2 on S, and then ends with 'Mais ... mal !!'. Reconstruction:
- the lifts ±λ_0 both give unipotent elements only if trace 2 = −2, i.e. 4 = 0;
- so uniqueness holds when 2 is invertible, and trivially when 2 = 0 (where −1 = 1);
- it fails when 2 is neither invertible nor zero, e.g. over Z_(2) or non-reduced bases;
- hence the exclamation.
This is local bookkeeping in a long computation.
